# PROMPT_INSTRUCTIONS). Distinct from recall@k, which only ever measures
# whether existing content was found: this measures whether the system
# honestly admits when a named entity has nothing relevant covering it,
# instead of silently answering only the part it can.
#
# The question names two entities: atlas-project (well covered, five
# events) and beacon-project (declared, so CueExtractor can find it as a
# real cue - four events mention it, none relevant to this question).
#
# First draft used a single Beacon event. Dropped before trusting any
# result: `fanAttenuatedStrength(fan)` = 1/(1+(fan-1)^2) gives a
# uniquely-mentioned entity (fan=1) the maximum possible spreading
# score (1.0), and GRAPH_DISCOVERY_WEIGHT adds that directly and
# undiluted to _memoryScore - so a single Beacon mention ranked first by
# a wide margin, confirmed live. Structurally, that's correct behavior
# (finding the one thing that exists about a rare, specifically-named
# entity should rank highly), not a bug - but it means a single mention
# is never a real "gap" case for this mechanism. Four Beacon events
# bring its fan to a strength the atlas-project cue's own fan (5 events,
# ~0.06) is already comparable to, so the real discriminator becomes
# semantic relevance, where these four score near zero against this
# question.
#
# All events share one timestamp, learning the same lesson atlas-02 did -
# recency differences would confound which events win the top-3 slots.

